**Current versus accepted.** Alpha scaffolds use declaration-owned `#Config of T`
inputs with grouped parenthesised config and direct builder slot contributions.
The accepted `$config`/`$project`/`$html_builder` forms are queued. A general
`$entry` purpose is deferred; the earlier `$page` proposal is superseded.
`moth new` does not emit the queued directives yet.

A Moth project is the directory you select. That directory is both the build
root and the source root. There is no implicit `src/` folder.

### Accepted starter config

The queued directive form leaves version optional and has no `entry_root`:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    $project(
        name = "my_site",
    )

    $html_builder
]

### Where things live

- `config.moth` at the selected root is optional and controls build settings.
- Everything below the selected root is source unless it is excluded or is a generated hidden output.
- `_dev/` and `_release/` are generated output. Do not edit them.

### Up to two root files per module directory

Each source directory can have one `@*.moth` normal root and one `+*.moth`
support root. They form one structural module with shared helper files and two
isolated interfaces. The text after `@` stays cosmetic, so `@page.moth`,
`@api.moth` and `@mod.moth` all create the same root role. A normal root becomes
an HTML artefact from authored root activity; a direct builder slot
contribution is const-fragment activity:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    #[$in title:Welcome]
]

A future `$entry` purpose will make that selection explicit. It is deferred and
is not current syntax; do not write `$entry` or `$page`.

The current scaffold's `@page.moth` filename doesn't make the filename a page
or indicate that a root-purpose directive is present.

### Shared scoped packages

A `+*.moth` root creates a scoped support surface. For example,
`shared/+package.moth` creates `@shared`. Its owner module, normal sibling
modules and their descendants may depend on it. Other support packages in the
same scope and the package's private implementation subtree cannot. When a
directory has both roots, helpers are shared while each root keeps a separate
authoring scope and public interface. A parent module selects a paired child's
`@` surface; eligible sibling branches select its `+` surface. The parent never
falls back to support exports. A project-root `+*.moth` is selected by project
package assembly as the external package surface.

### Outputs are generated

The build command creates hidden output folders. They are not source. The
builder cleans up stale artefacts automatically.
